---
// "How it works" band: grilling callout + the discuss/plan/execute/verify cycle.
---

<section class="band">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">How it works</span>
    <h2 class="h2">Resolve one ticket at a time</h2>
    <p class="sub">Chart a map from a loose idea. Anything not sharp enough to plan yet sits in the <b style="color:var(--fg)">fog</b>; a grilling conversation dissolves it into decided tickets on the <b style="color:var(--fg)">frontier</b>. Resolve one per session and the map grows toward the destination.</p>
    <div class="callout">
      <div class="qmark">?</div>
      <div>
        <h3>Grilling clears the fog</h3>
        <p>trailhead won't guess the shape of your project. It <b>interrogates you one pointed question at a time</b>, converging a fuzzy area of the map into a closed decision, then writes it up as a real ticket. That's how the fog turns into work you can actually build, never an agent quietly deciding for you.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cycle">
      <div class="step"><div class="n">01</div><h3>Discuss</h3><p>Grill the idea into closed choices, only when there's real ambiguity.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="n">02</div><h3>Plan</h3><p>Steps, seams, files, and verification criteria, reviewable on the ticket.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="n">03</div><h3>Execute</h3><p>Atomic commits that each reference the ticket, visible in its timeline.</p></div>
      <div class="step"><div class="n">04</div><h3>Verify</h3><p>Tests, adversarial code review, and acceptance before the ticket closes.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="frontier-note">
      <span class="chip"><b>Frontier</b> — open, unblocked tickets you can take now</span>
      <span class="chip"><b>Fog</b> — what isn't sharp enough to plan yet</span>
      <span class="chip"><b>Destination</b> — a working artifact, not a spec</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
